---
title: 'épisode 15 : on casse la croute'
tags:
  - fr
  - language
  - learning
---

Q: What does `casser la croute` mean?
A: To have a meal or something to eat, often a simple meal.
N: The episode title introduces eating together. Manuel p. 108. [[fr/episode-15]]

---

Q: What is `un repas-partage`?
A: A meal where each person brings a dish to share, a potluck.
N: Each person contributes food. Manuel p. 109. [[fr/episode-15]]

---

Q: What does the restaurant formula `apportez votre vin` mean?
A: Bring your own wine.
N: Invitation B uses this restaurant formula. Manuel p. 109. [[fr/episode-15]]

---

Q: Complete with a partitive article: `Dans ce sandwich, il y a ___ poulet.`
A: {{`du`}}
N: `Poulet` is masculine singular. Manuel p. 110. [[fr/episode-15]]

---

Q: Complete with a partitive article: `Il y a ___ mayonnaise.`
A: {{`de la`}}
N: `Mayonnaise` is feminine singular. Manuel p. 110. [[fr/episode-15]]

---

Q: Complete with a partitive article: `Dans la vinaigrette, il y a ___ huile.`
A: {{`de l'`}}
N: Elision before the silent h in `huile`. Manuel p. 111. [[fr/episode-15]]

---

Q: Complete with an indefinite plural article: `Dans ce sandwich, il y a ___ œufs.`
A: {{`des`}}
N: `Œufs` is plural. Manuel p. 110. [[fr/episode-15]]

---

Q: Say “a cup of milk” in French.
A: {{`Une tasse de lait.`}}
N: Use `de` after this quantity expression. Manuel p. 110. [[fr/episode-15]]

---

Q: Say “a little lemon juice” in French.
A: {{`Un peu de jus de citron.`}}
N: Use `de` after `un peu`. Manuel p. 111. [[fr/episode-15]]

---

Q: Say “I like cheese” in French.
A: {{`J'aime le fromage.`}}
N: A general taste takes the definite article. Manuel p. 112. [[fr/episode-15]]

---

Q: Make `J'aime le fromage` negative.
A: {{`Je n'aime pas le fromage.`}}
N: The definite article `le` stays after `aimer`. [[fr/episode-15]]

---

Q: Make `Je mange du fromage` negative, meaning “I don't eat any cheese.”
A: {{`Je ne mange pas de fromage.`}}
N: In this negative sentence, the partitive becomes `de`. [[fr/episode-15]]

---

Q: Conjugate `préférer` in the present with `je`.
A: {{`Je préfère.`}}
N: Accent grave in the stressed stem. Manuel p. 113. [[fr/episode-15]]

---

Q: Conjugate `préférer` in the present with `nous`.
A: {{`Nous préférons.`}}
N: Retain `é` in `préférons`. Manuel p. 113. [[fr/episode-15]]

---

Q: Conjugate `préférer` in the present with `ils`.
A: {{`Ils préfèrent.`}}
N: The ending `-ent` is silent. Manuel p. 113. [[fr/episode-15]]

---

Q: Ask “What is your favourite dish?” using `tu` and `Quel est`.
A: {{`Quel est ton plat préféré ?`}}
N: `Plat` is masculine: `quel`, `ton`, `préféré`. Manuel p. 113. [[fr/episode-15]]

---

Q: Ask “What is your favourite drink?” using `tu` and `Quelle est`.
A: {{`Quelle est ta boisson préférée ?`}}
N: `Boisson` is feminine: `quelle`, `ta`, `préférée`. Manuel p. 113. [[fr/episode-15]]

---

Q: Ask “Which dish do you prefer?” using inversion with `tu`.
A: {{`Quel plat préfères-tu ?`}}
N: Retain the hyphen in `préfères-tu`. Manuel p. 113. [[fr/episode-15]]

---

Q: Ask the closed question “Do you like tea?” using `est-ce que` and `tu`.
A: {{`Est-ce que tu aimes le thé ?`}}
N: A yes/no answer is possible. [[fr/episode-15]]

---

Q: Ask the open question “What do you like to eat?” using `qu'est-ce que` and `tu`.
A: {{`Qu'est-ce que tu aimes manger ?`}}
N: Answer with a food or dish. [[fr/episode-15]]

---

Q: Say “I love strawberries” using `adorer`.
A: {{`J'adore les fraises.`}}
N: `Adorer` expresses a strong positive taste. Manuel p. 112. [[fr/episode-15]]

---

Q: Say “I hate cheese” using `détester`.
A: {{`Je déteste le fromage.`}}
N: CD2 piste 38, letter b. Manuel p. 112. [[fr/episode-15]]

---

Q: Say “I don't like salad at all” using `pas du tout`.
A: {{`Je n'aime pas du tout la salade.`}}
N: The definite article remains after a verb of taste. Manuel p. 112. [[fr/episode-15]]

---

Q: Why can `C'est écœurant !` describe either good or bad food in Québec?
A: Depending on the context and tone, it can express disgust or strong enthusiasm.
N: The exercise accepts both B and PB. Manuel p. 111. [[fr/episode-15]]

---

Q: Say “It's delicious!” in French.
A: {{`C'est délicieux !`}}
N: A positive comment on a dish. Manuel p. 111. [[fr/episode-15]]

---

Q: Does `Ouach !` express enjoyment or disgust about food?
A: Disgust.
N: Classified PB, `pas bon`, in the exercise. Manuel p. 111. [[fr/episode-15]]

---

Q: Say “blueberries” in Québec French, with the plural definite article.
A: {{`Les bleuets.`}}
N: Food vocabulary in the episode. [[fr/episode-15]]

---

Q: Say “watermelon” in Québec French, with its definite article.
A: {{`Le melon d'eau.`}}
N: Food vocabulary in the episode. [[fr/episode-15]]

---

Q: Say “a pinch of salt” in French.
A: {{`Une pincée de sel.`}}
N: A quantity expression used in recipes. Manuel p. 110. [[fr/episode-15]]

---

Q: What are the three main ingredients of the poutine described in the episode?
A: {{`Des frites, du fromage en grains et de la sauce brune.`}}
N: Recipe vocabulary. Manuel p. 110. [[fr/episode-15]]

---

Q: Say “I'll take care of dessert” using the present of `s'occuper de`.
A: {{`Je m'occupe du dessert.`}}
N: `De + le` becomes `du`. The present can express an arrangement. Manuel p. 108. [[fr/episode-15]]

---

Q: Say “We're meeting around eleven” using `se donner rendez-vous` and `on`.
A: {{`On se donne rendez-vous vers onze heures.`}}
N: `Vers` gives an approximate time. Manuel p. 108. [[fr/episode-15]]
